1858:`time.now()` is a record of `seconds` and `nanoseconds` since
1859-1970-01-01T00:00:00Z, and `time.sleep` answers `nothing` once the pause is over.
1860-`time.format` takes a date value or a number of seconds since the same instant
1861-and a format string; with no format string it writes `%Y-%m-%d %H:%M:%S`.
1862-`time.parse` reads an ISO-8601 timestamp as a date, applying any zone offset so
1863-the value is the UTC reading of the instant. `time.parse(text, layout)` reads
1864-text in a caller-chosen layout instead, the shape another system emits.
1865-`time.unix` is the other direction:
1866-it takes a timestamp text or a date value and answers the seconds since the same
1867-instant, read as UTC wherever it runs. A date that holds a fractional second
1868-answers a fractional number of seconds rather than dropping it, so
1869-`time.unix(time.parse("2024-02-29T23:59:58.5Z"))` is `1709251198.5`.
1870-`time.from_unix(seconds)` is the inverse and turns such seconds into a date
1871-value. A fractional number is truncated toward zero, the answer this builtin
1872-gave before `time.parse` existed, so `time.from_unix(-0.5)` is
1873-`1970-01-01 00:00:00` and `time.from_unix(-1.9)` is `1969-12-31 23:59:59`.
1874-Truncation applies only to converting a number into the legacy whole-second
1875-date; a date value that holds a fraction is still returned as a fractional
